"Rast" <rast2@hotmail.com> wrote:

> I meant, "why give any points at all for killing
> monsters?"

1) For players in the early learning stages of
NetHack, "how many and how tough a set of monsters
can you kill before they kill you" is a perfectly
valid measure of game proficiency progress, and
so a score based on kills allows both for
competition with oneself, and for competition with
others at similar skill levels.

2) Unlike a scoring system based on a few dozen
subgoals, the system based on kills provides for a
finely graduated score, avoiding one other
commentator's issue with "frequent ties".  The
entire range of a 31 bit integer is available as
achievable scores.

By the coincidence math of the birthday paradox,
that doesn't, cannot, at all assure that all the
world's NetHack players have different highest
scores ever from one another, but it certainly helps
at the single NetHack game server level where most
score-table comparisions are done.

